5.1 if-statements
In R, the starting point to conditional execution is the if statement, with the following structure:

if (condition) {
  # If the condition is TRUE, do this
}

Here condition is a logical expression (e.g., x &gt; 0) that determines whether the code inside the curly bracket { } is executed. Typically, if-statements are part of a longer analysis script or a function, where actions depend on the result of the earlier steps, for example:

year &lt;- 2025

if (year == 2025) {
  print("Year is 2025")
}

[1] "Year is 2025"


Had we assigned anything other than 2025 to the variable year, nothing would’ve been printed:

year &lt;- 2024

if (year == 2025) {
  print("It's year 2025")
}

This basic structure is the first building block for constructing more complex conditional executions. In what follows, we move on to structures that can handle the other side of the condition, that is, it allows us to determine what happens when the condition is FALSE. The if-statement and its relative are called control flow tools, as they are used to control how a statistical program adapts to data, which resembles the way a statistician adapts their inferences based on what is observed in the data!

Using if-statements with next and break to control for-loops
At times, we want greater control inside a for-loop to make it more flexible. For example, to prevent errors during iteration, we may want to skip a step, exit a loop early, or modify the loop’s behavior based on certain conditions. if-statements can be used together with the following commands to control the flow from inside the loop:

next: Skip to the next iteration.
This statement skips the current iteration and moves on to the next one. It is useful when you want to ignore certain values without stopping the loop entirely. For example, you can use it to skip over missing values:

x &lt;- c(1,2, NA, 4, 5)

for(i in 1:length(x)){
  if(is.na(x[i])){
    next # Skip this iteration
  }
  print(x[i])
}

[1] 1
[1] 2
[1] 4
[1] 5


break: Exit the loop early.
This statement stops the loop entirely and moves on to the code after the loop. It is useful when, for example, you are searching through data and want to stop once a certain condition is met and continuing the loop no longer makes sense. For example, it can be used to stop the loop whenever the object in the loop exceeds a certain threshold:

x &lt;- c(5, 20, 50, 120, 150)

for(i in 1:length(x)){
  if(x[i] &gt; 100){
    break # Exits the loop 
  }
  print(x[i])
}

[1] 5
[1] 20
[1] 50



The next and break statements allow you to build more flexible and data-responsive loops that, for example, skip over bad inputs or exit the loop once a desired condition is met. They are especially useful when writing your own functions - a topic we’ll get to shortly!

5.3 if-elseif-else-statements
In practice, it’s often enough to evaluate a binary condition: if something is true, do one thing; else, do another. However, many real-world statistical decisions involve more than two possible outcomes. For instance, based on a test statistic, we might want to say whether there is very strong evidence, moderate evidence, or no evidence against a null hypothesis. This kind of multi-branch logic is handled in R using the else if clause, which extend the basic if-else structure.
The general syntax is:

if (condition) {
  # Execute this code if condition is TRUE
} else if (condition2) {
  # Execute this code if condition1 is FALSE and condition2 is TRUE
} else {
  # Execute this code if both condition1 and condition2 are FALSE
}

You can add as many else if-clauses as you want:

if (condition) {
  # Execute this code if condition is TRUE
} else if (condition2) {
  # Execute this code if condition1 is FALSE and condition2 is TRUE
} else if (condition3) {
  # Execute this code if condition1 and condition2 are FALSE, but condition3 is TRUE
} ... # bunch of else ifs
else if (conditionN){
  # Execute this condition if conditions 1,...,N-1 are FALSE, but conditionN is TRUE
} else {
  # Execute this condition if all N conditions are FALSE
}

To continue with our previous example, suppose you’re interpreting a standardized test statistic (e.g., a z-score) and want to classify the strength of the evidence based on common critical values for 95% and 99% confidence:

ratio = 2.8 # Here, we hard-code it, but in a real-life scenario you'd compute it from data

if (abs(ratio) &gt; 2.6) {
  print("The evidence suggests that the mean significantly differs from 0 at the 99% confidence level.")
} else if (abs(ratio) &gt; 2) {
  print("The evidence suggests that the mean significantly differs from 0 at the 95% confidence level.")
} else {
  print("No strong evidence against a mean of zero.")
}

[1] "The evidence suggests that the mean significantly differs from 0 at the 99% confidence level."


This kind of structure is especially useful when reporting results, as it allows you to assign labels to different test outcomes depending on the category they fall in.

8.3 The ellipsis ...
The ... (ellipsis) argument allows a function to accept an arbitrary number of additional arguments and pass them along to other functions it calls internally. This way you can make your function more flexible and reusable. Consider the following example, which is another version of the function center_func() we wrote in the last module:

center_func &lt;- function(x, center = TRUE, ...) {
  if( center ) {
    return(x - mean(x, ...))
  }
  return(x)
}

center_func(c(1,2,3, NA), na.rm = TRUE)

[1] -1  0  1 NA


We know that mean() has the default argument na.rm = FALSE, so using the ellipsis ..., we can pass this as an argument to center_func(), even if it doesn’t have that argument. In fact, it can hold any arguments that are either used or not:

center_func(c(1,2,3, NA), na.rm = TRUE, 
            random_argument1 = "This will not be used", 
            random_argument2 = "Neither will this")

[1] -1  0  1 NA


This approach is extremely useful when writing wrappers or utility functions where flexibility matters, such as plotting, modeling, or summarizing functions, because this way your function does not have to have as separate arguments all of the possible optional arguments of the functions it calls. You can even capture the ellipsis into a list:

center_func &lt;- function(x, center = TRUE, ...) {
  args = list(...)
  print(args)
  if( center ) {
    return(x - mean(x, ...))
  }
  return(x)
}

center_func(c(1,2,3, NA), na.rm = TRUE, 
            random_argument1 = "This will not be used", 
            random_argument2 = "Neither will this")

$na.rm
[1] TRUE

$random_argument1
[1] "This will not be used"

$random_argument2
[1] "Neither will this"


[1] -1  0  1 NA
